    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d817">

                <pb facs="1279" n="1265" sameAs="frus:frus1944v01/pg1265"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00116 European War 1939/1238: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Winant</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-01-13T19:00:00-04:00">January 13, 1944—7
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>329. It is not quite clear from your 163, January 7, 7 p.m. whether you intended
                    to suggest that the Department should broach this subject <pb facs="1280"
                        n="1266" xml:id="pg1266"/> to the Soviet Government.<anchor n="6"
                        xml:id="d817-f1"/> In any event the Department doubts the wisdom of its
                    taking any action at this time.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d817-f1" n="6" xml:id="d817-f1-content">
                <p>In telegram 349, January 14, 4 p.m., the Ambassador replied that telegram 163 was
                    forwarded simply for information (740.00116 European War 1939/1750).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
